<bp:ProteinReference rdf:about="http://identifiers.org/uniprot/P51580">
 <bp:entityFeature rdf:resource="http://pathwaycommons.org/pc12/ModificationFeature_93f89802915976d59a474cf4a0741722" />
 <bp:entityFeature rdf:resource="http://pathwaycommons.org/pc12/ModificationFeature_ed32af70afbc39d77f7fb9be921877fe" />
 <bp:entityFeature rdf:resource="http://pathwaycommons.org/pc12/ModificationFeature_953221ed2fc98d1035cd90e6d0b7d272" />
 <bp:entityFeature rdf:resource="http://pathwaycommons.org/pc12/ModificationFeature_79da07d43c07d274f785f8e66e099a3e" />
 <bp:standardName rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Thiopurine S-methyltransferase</bp:standardName>
 <bp:xref rdf:resource="http://pathwaycommons.org/pc12/UnificationXref_uniprot_knowledgebase_P51580" />
 <bp:xref rdf:resource="http://pathwaycommons.org/pc12/RelationshipXref_hgnc_symbol_TPMT_identity" />
 <bp:organism rdf:resource="http://identifiers.org/taxonomy/9606" />
 <bp:displayName rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">TPMT_HUMAN</bp:displayName>
 <bp:name rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Thiopurine methyltransferase</bp:name>
 <bp:name rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">2.1.1.67</bp:name>
 <bp:name rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">TPMT</bp:name>
 <bp:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">REPLACED http://biocyc.org/biopax/biopax-level3ProteinReference80257</bp:comment>
 <bp:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">REPLACED http://pathwaycommons.org/pc12/ProteinReference_rcsb_pdb_2BZG_see-also</bp:comment>
 <bp:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">REPLACED http://mirtarbase.mbc.nctu.edu.tw/#ref_7172</bp:comment>
 <bp:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">REPLACED http://www.ctdbase.org/#ref_protein_gene_7172</bp:comment>
 <bp:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">REPLACED http://identifiers.org/refseq/NP_000358</bp:comment>
 <bp:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">FUNCTION: Catalyzes the S-methylation of thiopurine drugs such as6-mercaptopurine (also called mercaptopurine, 6-MP or its brandname Purinethol) and 6-thioguanine (also called tioguanine or 6-TG) using S-adenosyl-L-methionine as the methyl donor(PubMed:657528, PubMed:18484748). TPMT activity modulates thecytotoxic effects of thiopurine prodrugs. A natural substrate forthis enzyme has yet to be identified.{ECO:0000269|PubMed:18484748, ECO:0000269|PubMed:657528,ECO:0000305}.CATALYTIC ACTIVITY:Reaction=S-adenosyl-L-methionine + a thiopurine = S-adenosyl-L-homocysteine + a thiopurine S-methylether.; EC=2.1.1.67;Evidence={ECO:0000269|PubMed:18484748,ECO:0000269|PubMed:657528};CATALYTIC ACTIVITY:Reaction=mercaptopurine + S-adenosyl-L-methionine = 6-methylthiopurine + H(+) + S-adenosyl-L-homocysteine;Xref=Rhea:RHEA:12609, ChEBI:CHEBI:15378, ChEBI:CHEBI:28279,ChEBI:CHEBI:50667, ChEBI:CHEBI:57856, ChEBI:CHEBI:59789;EC=2.1.1.67; Evidence={ECO:0000269|PubMed:18484748,ECO:0000269|PubMed:657528};CATALYTIC ACTIVITY:Reaction=6-thioguanine + S-adenosyl-L-methionine = 6-methylthioguanine + H(+) + S-adenosyl-L-homocysteine;Xref=Rhea:RHEA:56580, ChEBI:CHEBI:9555, ChEBI:CHEBI:15378,ChEBI:CHEBI:57856, ChEBI:CHEBI:59789, ChEBI:CHEBI:140528;EC=2.1.1.67; Evidence={ECO:0000269|PubMed:657528};ACTIVITY REGULATION: Inhibited by S-adenosyl-L-homocysteine (SAH).BIOPHYSICOCHEMICAL PROPERTIES:Kinetic parameters:KM=18.5 uM for S-adenosyl-L-methionine (at pH 6.5 and 37 degreesCelsius) {ECO:0000269|PubMed:18484748};KM=0.68 mM for 6-mercaptopurine (at pH 6.5 and 37 degreesCelsius) {ECO:0000269|PubMed:18484748};KM=1.7 uM for S-adenosyl-L-methionine (at pH 7.5 and 37 degreesCelsius) {ECO:0000269|PubMed:657528};KM=0.32 mM for 6-mercaptopurine (at pH 7.5 and 37 degreesCelsius) {ECO:0000269|PubMed:657528};KM=0.20 mM for 6-thioguanine (at pH 7.5 and 37 degrees Celsius){ECO:0000269|PubMed:657528};Vmax=1.23 nmol/sec/mg enzyme toward 6-mercaptopurine (at pH 6.5and 37 degrees Celsius) {ECO:0000269|PubMed:18484748};pH dependence:Optimum pH is 7.5. {ECO:0000269|PubMed:657528};SUBUNIT: Monomer. {ECO:0000269|PubMed:17243178}.SUBCELLULAR LOCATION: Cytoplasm.POLYMORPHISM: Polymorphic variations define TPMT activity levelsthat are variable among ethnic groups. 90% of Caucasians have highTPMT activity, 10% have intermediate activity, and 1 in 300individuals has low activity (PubMed:10208641). These differencesinfluence the clinical use and therapeutic efficacy of thiopurinedrugs, generally used as immunosuppressants or cytotoxic drugs inconditions including leukemia, autoimmune disease and organtransplantation. Intermediate or low TPMT activity is associatedwith thiopurine intolerance and patients are at risk of toxicityafter receiving standard doses of thiopurine drugs [MIM:610460](PubMed:10751626, PubMed:15819814, PubMed:16220112,PubMed:16476125, PubMed:16789994, PubMed:7862671, PubMed:8561894,PubMed:8644731, PubMed:9246020, PubMed:9336428, PubMed:9711875,PubMed:9931345, PubMed:9931346). The most prevalent TPMT allelesassociated with TPMT deficiency are TPMT*2 and TPMT*3A. Theproteins encoded by TPMT*2 and TPMT*3A mutant are degraded morerapidly by an ATP-dependent proteasome-mediated pathway(PubMed:9177237, PubMed:8644731). {ECO:0000269|PubMed:10208641,ECO:0000269|PubMed:10751626, ECO:0000269|PubMed:15819814,ECO:0000269|PubMed:16220112, ECO:0000269|PubMed:16476125,ECO:0000269|PubMed:16789994, ECO:0000269|PubMed:7862671,ECO:0000269|PubMed:8561894, ECO:0000269|PubMed:8644731,ECO:0000269|PubMed:9177237, ECO:0000269|PubMed:9246020,ECO:0000269|PubMed:9336428, ECO:0000269|PubMed:9711875,ECO:0000269|PubMed:9931345, ECO:0000269|PubMed:9931346}.POLYMORPHISM: TPMT*3A is the most common allele in the Caucasiansand American Caucasians; it is the only mutant allele found in theSouth West Asians; it is not found in the Chinese. TPMT*3C iscommon in African-Americans and is the only allele in Chinese,Japanese and Taiwanese individuals. This allele is found at a lowfrequency in the Caucasians. This suggests that TPMT*3C is theoldest mutation, with TPMT*3B being acquired later to form theTPMT*3A allele in the Caucasian and South West Asian populations.TPMT*2 appears to be a more recent allele, which has only beendetected in Caucasians to date. {ECO:0000269|PubMed:10208641,ECO:0000269|PubMed:10751626, ECO:0000269|PubMed:16476125}.SIMILARITY: Belongs to the class I-like SAM-bindingmethyltransferase superfamily. TPMT family. {ECO:0000305}.SEQUENCE CAUTION:Sequence=AAB71631.1; Type=Erroneous initiation; Evidence={ECO:0000305};Sequence=AAB71632.1; Type=Erroneous initiation; Evidence={ECO:0000305}; COPYRIGHT: UniProt Consortium (www.uniprot.org). Distributed under the Creative Commons Attribution-NoDerivs License.</bp:comment>
 <bp:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">TPMT_HUMAN Reviewed; 245 AA.</bp:comment>
</bp:ProteinReference>

